 to estimate the parameters reliably.\n- The assumptions of normality and homoscedasticity can be reasonably met.\nSelection criteria include the nature of the data, the underlying theoretical framework, and the complexity of the relationship being modeled.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of parametric functions typically involves:\n1. Specifying the functional form of the model.\n2. Estimating the parameters using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) for linear models or Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) for more complex models.\n3. Evaluating the goodness-of-fit using statistical tests (e.g., R-squared, F-tests) and diagnostic plots.\n4. Conducting hypothesis tests on the parameters to assess their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Parametric functions can provide a clear and interpretable model of relationships between variables.\n- They often require fewer data points to estimate parameters compared to non-parametric methods.\n- They allow for the incorporation of prior knowledge about the relationship through the choice of functional form.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The primary limitation of parametric functions is their reliance on the correct specification of the functional form; incorrect specifications can lead to biased estimates and misleading conclusions.\n- They may not perform well with small sample sizes or when the assumptions of normality and homoscedasticity are violated.\n- They can be less flexible than non-parametric methods, which do not assume a specific functional form.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include non-parametric functions, which do not assume a specific form and can be more flexible in modeling relationships. Examples include kernel smoothing and spline regression. Additionally, generalized linear models (GLMs) extend parametric functions to accommodate non-normal response distributions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen im